WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.195
Here's something nobody
prints on the label.

2
00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:05.070
Encryption has a shelf life.

3
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:12.520
Traffic recorded today can be opened later,
once a quantum computer is big enough.

4
00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:17.606
So the board asks a fair question.

5
00:00:17.646 --> 00:00:20.819
When do we switch to
quantum-safe cryptography?

6
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.107
And the honest answer
begins with an awkward one.

7
00:00:27.147 --> 00:00:29.359
We don't actually know where it is.

8
00:00:32.617 --> 00:00:34.960
It's in code. In configuration.

9
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.383
In certificates. In a server
nobody has touched in years.

10
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:44.223
Meet Acxelin QReadiness.

11
00:00:44.263 --> 00:00:49.781
It finds the cryptography you depend on,
tells you what a quantum computer, or an

12
00:00:49.821 --> 00:00:53.897
attacker today, could break,
and ranks what to change first.

13
00:00:53.937 --> 00:00:56.750
For security and engineering teams alike.

14
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.706
Start with a repository.

15
00:01:01.746 --> 00:01:06.976
Pick it, press scan, and it gets to
work, right here on your own computer.

16
00:01:10.417 --> 00:01:14.123
Moments later, one number
leads: the things to fix now.

17
00:01:14.163 --> 00:01:19.437
Open any of them, and you see where it
is, why it matters, and exactly what to

18
00:01:19.477 --> 00:01:20.977
change.

19
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.764
Next, the servers you actually run.

20
00:01:32.804 --> 00:01:37.211
Type their addresses, and
each one gets a grade, A to F.

21
00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:43.860
An A means quantum-safe key exchange only.

22
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:49.059
A C means today's traffic could
be read later. Good to know first.

23
00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:54.152
Now make it someone's job.

24
00:01:54.192 --> 00:01:58.630
Give each fix an owner and a date,
and follow it through to done.

25
00:02:02.417 --> 00:02:06.035
And the overview keeps the
riskiest services right at the top.

26
00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:11.378
And when the auditors call, you're ready:

27
00:02:11.418 --> 00:02:15.449
a standard bill of materials for your
cryptography, and a one-page summary for the

28
00:02:15.489 --> 00:02:16.989
board.

29
00:02:20.683 --> 00:02:23.847
Curious what it looks for?
It shows you that too.

30
00:02:27.217 --> 00:02:31.635
It runs on your own machines.
Nothing is sent to Acxelin Quantum.

31
00:02:31.675 --> 00:02:35.311
And its advice follows NIST's
post-quantum standards.

32
00:02:42.217 --> 00:02:48.168
One honest note: cryptography a program
assembles while it runs can slip past.

33
00:02:48.208 --> 00:02:50.128
We'd rather tell you now.

34
00:02:52.217 --> 00:02:55.579
Acxelin QReadiness. Know where you stand.

